   <p>For teams spanning a wide enough spread of timezones — say, one participant in California and another in India, roughly 12-13 hours apart depending on daylight saving — a genuine, comfortable working-hours overlap may simply not exist. In this situation, the honest options are limited: someone attends slightly outside their normal working hours, the meeting gets kept deliberately short and tightly focused to minimize the inconvenience, or the interaction shifts to an asynchronous format that doesn't require everyone present simultaneously at all. Pretending a comfortable overlap exists when it genuinely doesn't tends to produce chronic frustration for whoever ends up bearing the inconvenient time slot repeatedly, which is exactly the situation the next section's rotation approach is specifically designed to address fairly.</p>
 
   <h2 id="rotating">Rotating the Inconvenient Slot Fairly</h2>
