     <tr><td>Fixed inconvenient time</td><td>Same person or region always attends outside normal hours — simple but unfair over time</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Rotating inconvenient time</td><td>The early/late slot moves between regions on a set schedule, spreading the burden</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Alternating meeting existence</td><td>Meeting alternates between two different times, so each region gets a convenient slot roughly half the time</td></tr>
   </table>
+  </div>
   <p>For genuinely recurring meetings where no single time works comfortably for everyone, explicitly rotating who bears the inconvenient slot is widely regarded as the fairer long-term approach, since it distributes the burden rather than consistently asking the same region or individual to accommodate everyone else indefinitely. This requires slightly more scheduling overhead than a single fixed time, but teams that adopt this practice consistently report it feels noticeably more equitable than the alternative of one region silently absorbing the inconvenience meeting after meeting without acknowledgment.</p>
 
