   <h2 id="formula">The Compound Interest Formula</h2>
   <p>The standard compound interest formula is: <code>A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt)</code>, where A is the final amount, P is the principal (starting amount), r is the annual interest rate (as a decimal), n is the number of times interest compounds per year, and t is the number of years. The exponent <code>nt</code> is the mathematical source of compound interest's accelerating growth — as t (time) increases, the exponent grows, and because it's an exponent rather than a simple multiplier, the growth compounds rather than accumulates linearly. This is the fundamental mathematical difference between compound growth and simple linear growth, and it's why the formula's output curves upward more and more steeply the longer the time period extends, rather than following a straight line. Understanding this formula's structure — specifically that time appears as an exponent rather than a simple multiplier — is the mathematical key to understanding every other insight in this guide about why time matters more than rate for long-term compound growth.</p>
 
   <h2 id="simple-vs-compound">Simple Interest vs. Compound Interest</h2>
-  <table class="bp-table">
+  <div class="table-wrap">
+    <table class="table">
     <tr><th>Interest Type</th><th>Calculated On</th><th>Growth Pattern</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Simple Interest</td><td>Original principal only, every period</td><td>Linear (constant amount added each period)</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Compound Interest</td><td>Principal plus all previously accumulated interest</td><td>Exponential (increasing amount added each period)</td></tr>
   </table>
+  </div>
   <p>The distinction is straightforward but has significant long-term consequences: with simple interest, $1,000 at 5% annually always earns exactly $50 per year, indefinitely, since the calculation base never changes. With compound interest, that same $1,000 at 5% earns $50 in year one, but in year two earns 5% of $1,050 (the original principal plus year one's interest), which is $52.50 — a small difference in year two, but one that compounds further in every subsequent year, producing meaningfully larger gaps over longer periods as the base amount being earned on keeps growing. Over a 30-year horizon, this seemingly small early difference compounds into a genuinely substantial gap between the two growth patterns, which is exactly why the distinction matters far more for long-term financial planning than it might initially appear from looking at just the first few years side by side.</p>
 
