   <p>Web pages aren't the only place Base64 image encoding shows up regularly. JSON, a purely text-based data format, has no native way to represent binary image data either — which is why APIs that need to send or receive image data as part of a JSON payload (rather than as a separate file upload) commonly use Base64 to represent that image within the JSON structure. This is common in scenarios like mobile app APIs sending a profile picture alongside other user data in a single request, or webhook payloads that include a screenshot or generated image as part of a larger JSON response. The same size and encoding tradeoffs apply here as they do for HTML embedding — Base64 within a JSON payload is a practical solution for keeping binary data inside a text-based format, at the cost of the same roughly 33% size increase.</p>
 
