   <p>DPI (dots per inch) determines how many pixels are rendered per inch of the original page, which directly controls both visual sharpness and file size. Screens generally display content at a much lower effective resolution than print, which is why 72 DPI — a legacy standard originally tied to old screen resolutions — remains adequate for on-screen viewing despite sounding low. Print, by contrast, genuinely benefits from higher DPI, since printed output reveals resolution limitations far more readily than a screen does, especially at larger physical sizes.</p>
 
