   <h2 id="syntax">The box-shadow Syntax Broken Down</h2>
   <p>A full box-shadow declaration looks like: <code>box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px 0px rgba(0,0,0,0.25);</code>. Reading left to right, the four numeric values are horizontal offset, vertical offset, blur radius, and spread radius, followed by the shadow's color (here using rgba to include transparency). An optional <code>inset</code> keyword can appear at the start or end of the value to flip the shadow from outer to inner. Multiple shadows can be layered by separating complete shadow declarations with commas, a technique covered in more detail further down. The order of the four numeric values is fixed and cannot be rearranged — browsers parse box-shadow strictly according to this positional convention, meaning even a syntactically valid-looking value with values in the wrong order will produce an unexpected visual result rather than an error, since the browser has no way to know your intended meaning differs from the standard positional interpretation.</p>
 
   <h2 id="offset">Horizontal and Vertical Offset</h2>
-  <table class="bp-table">    <tr><th>Value</th><th>What It Controls</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
+  <div class="table-wrap">
+    <table class="table">    <tr><th>Value</th><th>What It Controls</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Horizontal offset</td><td>Left/right shadow position</td><td>Positive moves shadow right, negative moves it left</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Vertical offset</td><td>Up/down shadow position</td><td>Positive moves shadow down, negative moves it up</td></tr>
   </table>
+  </div>
   <p>These first two values position the shadow relative to the element casting it, functioning conceptually like a light source's direction — a shadow offset down and to the right suggests a light source coming from the upper left, which is the most common, natural-feeling convention used in most UI design, mimicking how light typically falls in real-world environments. Setting both offsets to zero produces a shadow that surrounds the element evenly on all sides rather than appearing to fall in any particular direction, useful for a subtle, ambient glow effect rather than a directional shadow suggesting elevation. Larger offset values combined with proportionally larger blur generally suggest a more dramatically elevated element, since real-world shadows genuinely do grow larger and more diffuse the further their source is lifted above the surface behind it, making offset magnitude a useful, intuitive dial for communicating relative elevation across different interface elements.</p>
 
