   <h2 id="inclusive">Inclusive vs. Exclusive Day Counting</h2>
   <p>This distinction causes more real-world confusion than any other part of date-difference calculation. A pure subtraction — end date minus start date — gives the number of days that have elapsed between the two dates, which excludes one of the two endpoints from the count. If you're counting from January 1 to January 5, this exclusive method gives 4 days. But many everyday contexts actually want an inclusive count, treating both the start and end date as part of the period being measured — a hotel stay from January 1 to January 5 that includes both check-in and check-out days as part of the stay would be described as "5 days," not 4. Neither convention is universally "correct" — they answer genuinely different questions, and the right one depends entirely on what the calculation is actually being used for. Rental periods, billing cycles, and vacation day counts often use inclusive counting; pure elapsed-time calculations (how long ago something happened) typically use exclusive counting. Getting this distinction wrong doesn't produce an obviously broken result — it produces a plausible-looking number that's simply off by one, which is exactly what makes this particular mistake so easy to make and so easy to overlook when reviewing a calculation afterward.</p>
 
