   <p>This distinction genuinely matters for anything involving business or professional timelines: a project quoted as "10 business days" takes roughly two calendar weeks to complete, not ten calendar days, since two weekend days out of every seven don't count toward the business-day total. Failing to account for this difference is a common source of missed expectations — someone expecting a "10 day" delivery counting calendar days will be surprised when it actually takes closer to two weeks if the quote was actually referring to business days all along. Always confirm explicitly which counting convention a quoted timeframe is using, especially for anything with real consequences like a contractual deadline or a shipping estimate, since the gap between the two conventions widens the longer the quoted period is — a "20 business day" estimate spans roughly four calendar weeks, nearly double what a naive calendar-day reading of the same number would suggest.</p>
 
