   <p>If text extraction returns nothing, or returns content that clearly doesn't match a text-based document (confirming the PDF is actually a scanned image), Optical Character Recognition becomes the relevant technology instead. OCR works fundamentally differently from text-layer extraction — rather than reading stored character data, it visually analyzes the image of each page and attempts to recognize individual characters and words based on their shapes, essentially the same task a human does when reading a scanned document, but automated. OCR accuracy depends heavily on scan quality, font clarity, and image resolution, and even high-quality OCR introduces some recognition errors that a genuine text layer never would, since it's fundamentally guessing at characters from pixels rather than reading stored data directly. Understanding this distinction helps set the right expectations: extraction from a real text layer is essentially always perfectly accurate, while OCR results always warrant a proofread pass before relying on them completely.</p>
 
