   <h2>Working with Extracted Text in Other Tools</h2>
   <p>Once text is out of a PDF, it typically becomes far more useful across other software than it was locked inside the original document. Plain text drops cleanly into a word processor for further editing, into a translation tool for converting to another language, into an AI system for summarization or analysis, or into a spreadsheet if the content includes structured data worth reorganizing into rows and columns. This portability is exactly the point of extraction — a PDF's own internal structure is optimized for consistent visual display across devices, not for being reused as raw material somewhere else, and converting to plain text removes that display-oriented packaging, leaving just the content itself, ready to be reshaped for whatever the next step actually requires.</p>
 
   <h2>Conclusion</h2>
   <p>For any PDF with a genuine text layer, extraction is instant, accurate, and completely private when it happens locally in your browser rather than through a server upload. The one hard limit worth internalizing is scanned documents — no text extraction tool can pull data that was never stored as text in the first place, and recognizing that distinction quickly saves the confusion of wondering why extraction "isn't working" on a document that was simply photographed rather than typed, pointing you toward OCR instead when that's genuinely what the task requires.</p>
