   <h2 id="why-surprising">Why Your Age in Days Feels So Much Bigger</h2>
   <p>The surprise people consistently feel seeing their age in days for the first time isn't a math error or a trick — it's simply what happens when the exact same span of elapsed time gets expressed in a much smaller unit. A year is a fairly large chunk of time, so counting in years produces small, familiar-feeling numbers. A day is a tiny fraction of a year by comparison, so counting the same span in days produces a number that's roughly 365 times larger for every year included. Thirty years, expressed in days, becomes something over 10,950 — a number that feels abstractly enormous next to the familiar "30," even though it represents exactly the same amount of lived time, just measured with a much finer-grained ruler. This is purely a psychological effect of scale, not a different or more "real" measurement of age — your age in years and your age in days describe identical elapsed time, just communicated through different-sized units.</p>
 
