   <h2 id="why-ratio">Why Aspect Ratio Matters More Than People Realize</h2>
   <p>Every platform that displays images enforces an aspect ratio, whether or not it says so explicitly. Upload a portrait photo to a platform expecting a landscape 16:9 frame, and the platform will typically auto-crop it for you — usually from the center, with no regard for what's actually the most important part of your composition. A carefully framed photo can end up with its subject's head cut off, simply because the platform's automatic crop didn't know what mattered in the frame. Cropping to the correct ratio yourself, deliberately, before uploading gives you control over exactly what stays in frame — control that an automatic platform crop won't give you, since no algorithm can reliably guess which part of a given photo the photographer actually intended to be the focal point.</p>
 
