   <h2>Conclusion</h2>
   <p>A lender's approval amount and your genuine, comfortable affordability are two different numbers answering two different questions, and confusing them is one of the more consequential financial mistakes borrowers make. Debt-to-income ratio is a useful, widely understood starting point for both perspectives, but treating the lender's maximum as your personal target overlooks everything DTI alone can't capture — your actual cost of living, income stability, and competing financial goals — all of which matter far more to your genuine day-to-day financial comfort than the number a lender's risk model happens to be willing to approve for you.</p>
