   <p>A meaningful chunk of meme culture relies on reusing a small set of highly recognizable template images — specific movie stills, reaction photos, or illustrations that have become shorthand for particular emotional beats, precisely because audiences already recognize the template and don't need the image itself explained. Using an original photo instead of a known template trades that instant recognition for something a template can't offer: specificity and personal relevance. A meme using your own photo of a specific person, pet, or situation lands differently for an audience who knows that context, even without the built-in recognition a famous template carries. Neither approach is inherently better — a recognizable template lowers the barrier to understanding for a broad audience, while an original image can land harder for an audience who shares the specific context, which is exactly the kind of meme this tool is built around.</p>
 
