   <p>Every PDF contains more than just visible page content — metadata like the document title, author, creation date, embedded fonts, and sometimes form fields or bookmarks. When merging, the visible page content from each source carries over faithfully, but document-level metadata (like the original title or author field) generally doesn't carry through in a meaningful combined way, since the new merged document is a genuinely new file with its own identity. If any of your source PDFs contain fillable form fields, be aware that complex interactive forms can sometimes behave differently after merging — for straightforward static documents (the overwhelming majority of everyday PDFs like scans, letters, and reports), this isn't a concern at all.</p>
 
