   <p>This is genuinely the more important factor in practice, and it's worth internalizing: even strong encryption is only as good as the password protecting it. A short, common, or predictable password (a name, a simple word, a short number sequence) can be guessed or brute-forced quickly regardless of how strong the underlying encryption algorithm is, since password-cracking tools work by trying many candidate passwords rapidly, not by attacking the encryption math directly in most practical scenarios. A long, random, unique password — ideally 12+ characters mixing letters, numbers, and symbols — dramatically increases the practical difficulty of cracking the file, often mattering more for real-world security than which specific encryption algorithm was used. Reusing a password you use elsewhere is also worth avoiding specifically for this use case, since a PDF is a file that might be shared, stored, or forwarded in ways an online account password wouldn't be, and a compromised PDF password doesn't put any of your actual accounts at risk in return, but a reused password does create that unnecessary link between the two.</p>
 
   <h2 id="two-passwords">Open Password vs. Permissions Password</h2>
-  <table class="bp-table">
+  <div class="table-wrap">
+    <table class="table">
     <tr><th>Password Type</th><th>What It Controls</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Open (User) Password</td><td>Required just to open and view the document at all — without it, the file cannot be read</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Permissions (Owner) Password</td><td>Allows viewing without a password, but restricts specific actions like printing, copying text, or editing unless the correct permissions password is provided</td></tr>
   </table>
+  </div>
   <p>These serve genuinely different purposes. An open password is appropriate when the document's content itself needs to stay confidential from anyone without authorization. A permissions password is appropriate when you're fine with people viewing the content but want to prevent specific actions — like discouraging casual copying of text from a document you're distributing, while still letting anyone open and read it. Many tools, including this one, focus specifically on the open password use case, since it's the more common need — genuinely restricting who can access the content at all, rather than restricting what an already-authorized viewer can do with it. It's worth noting that permissions restrictions are generally weaker in practice than open passwords, since a viewer who can already see the full content has more avenues to work around a copy or print restriction than someone who can't open the file at all.</p>
 
   <h2 id="how-it-works">How This Tool Encrypts Your File</h2>
   <p>The encryption happens entirely within your browser using JavaScript-based PDF encryption libraries capable of applying standard PDF encryption (RC4 128-bit) directly to the document's structure. Your password is used locally to derive the encryption key and encrypt the file's content; at no point is the password or the unencrypted document transmitted anywhere. This matters specifically for password protection tools more than almost any other PDF operation, since the entire point is keeping content confidential — a tool that uploads your file to a server to encrypt it defeats part of the purpose, since that server (and anyone with access to it) briefly sees your unencrypted content and your chosen password during that process, even if only momentarily. This is a structural guarantee rather than a policy promise: because the encryption computation happens using your browser's own JavaScript engine, there's no network request carrying your file or password to verify or audit — the privacy comes from the architecture itself, not from trusting a stated policy about what a server does or doesn't log.</p>
 
