+  </div>
   <p>This comparison is worth taking seriously: both major competitors use AES encryption, a meaningfully stronger and more modern standard than RC4, when applied through their server-based processing. This creates a genuine tradeoff — stronger encryption algorithm versus your file never leaving your device during the encryption process. For documents where the content's confidentiality during the encryption step itself is the primary concern (not wanting any server to see the unencrypted file, even briefly), local RC4 encryption may be preferable despite being a weaker algorithm. For documents requiring the strongest possible encryption against a genuinely sophisticated attacker and where trusting a reputable service's server-side process is acceptable, AES-based tools provide better protection. Neither choice is universally correct — it depends on which risk matters more for your specific document and threat model, and being able to reason through that tradeoff explicitly is more useful than simply defaulting to whichever tool is most familiar.</p>
 
