   <h2>Conclusion</h2>
   <p>Password protecting a PDF is genuinely effective at what it's actually designed to do — stopping casual, unauthorized access to a document's content — as long as expectations match the real strength of RC4 128-bit encryption and, more importantly, the strength of the password chosen. For everyday confidentiality needs, this level of protection paired with a strong password and entirely local processing is a solid, private choice; for documents facing a genuinely sophisticated threat, understanding the tradeoff against stronger server-processed alternatives helps make an informed decision rather than assuming all "password protected" PDFs offer identical security, regardless of which specific tool or algorithm sits behind that label.</p>
