   <h2 id="why-server">Why Server-Based Compression Is a Real Privacy Tradeoff</h2>
   <p>The most widely used PDF compressors — iLovePDF, SmallPDF, Adobe's online tool, and dozens of similar sites — all work the same fundamental way: you upload your file to their servers, their infrastructure processes it, and you download the result. This is a completely reasonable approach for a birthday invitation or a public flyer. It's a materially different question when the file is a resume with your home address and phone number, a signed contract, a bank statement, or a medical record — documents where you'd reasonably prefer not to introduce a third party into the chain of custody at all, even one with a good privacy policy.</p>
   <p>Most of these services are genuinely trustworthy and delete uploaded files after processing, as their privacy policies state. The distinction this guide is making isn't that they're untrustworthy — it's that browser-based processing removes the question entirely, rather than resolving it through a policy you have to trust. If your PDF is never transmitted anywhere, there's no server log, no temporary storage window, and no policy to rely on, because there was never a hand-off in the first place.</p>
 
