     <tr><td>Percentage increase</td><td>((new − old) ÷ old) × 100</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Percentage decrease</td><td>((old − new) ÷ old) × 100</td></tr>
   </table>
+  </div>
   <p>Both formulas are really the same calculation — the difference between two values, divided by the original (starting) value, expressed as a percentage. If a price rises from $80 to $100, the increase is ((100−80)÷80)×100 = 25%. If it then falls back from $100 to $80, the decrease is ((100−80)÷100)×100 = 20% — not 25%, since the base value used in the division is different in each direction. This asymmetry is one of the most common sources of confusion in percentage change calculations, and understanding why it happens (the denominator changes depending on which direction you're measuring) resolves most of the confusion at its root.</p>
 
