   <h2 id="why-limits">Why Exam Forms Enforce Tiny File Size Limits</h2>
   <p>A modern smartphone photo is typically 3-8MB. A typical exam portal photo requirement is 20KB to 100KB — often less than 2% of the original file size. This gap feels almost unreasonable until you understand what's actually driving it: government and exam portals process registrations from potentially millions of candidates within a narrow application window, and every megabyte of photo storage multiplied across that volume adds real infrastructure cost and real server load at exactly the moments (deadline days) when the portal needs to be most reliable. A strict, uniform size cap is a blunt but effective way to keep the system predictable under that load.</p>
   <p>This is why the requirement shows up almost identically across UPSC, SSC, IBPS, RRB NTPC, state PSC boards, NEET, JEE, and passport and visa applications — it's not one organization's arbitrary rule, it's a standard operational pattern across large-scale application processing.</p>
 
