   <p>Genuine, non-deterministic randomness requires deriving numbers from a physical process that's fundamentally unpredictable at a physical level, not just computationally difficult to predict. Sources used for true random number generation include atmospheric radio noise, radioactive decay timing, and various quantum-level physical phenomena, all of which are genuinely unpredictable in a way no deterministic algorithm, however complex, can ever fully replicate. Services specifically built around true randomness (rather than standard software pseudorandom generation) typically source their randomness from exactly these kinds of physical processes, and are used specifically in contexts where the theoretical predictability of pseudorandom generation, however unlikely to actually be exploited in practice, is nonetheless an unacceptable risk.</p>
 
   <h2 id="when-matters">When the Distinction Actually Matters</h2>
-  <table class="bp-table">
+  <div class="table-wrap">
+    <table class="table">
     <tr><th>Context</th><th>Pseudorandom OK?</th><th>Reasoning</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Games, simulations, casual selection</td><td>Yes</td><td>No one is trying to predict or exploit the sequence</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Statistical sampling, research</td><td>Yes</td><td>Statistical properties are what matter, not unpredictability against an adversary</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Cryptographic keys, security tokens</td><td>No</td><td>Predictability would represent a genuine, exploitable security vulnerability</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Lottery or gambling systems</td><td>Depends</td><td>Regulated contexts typically require certified true randomness or rigorously audited pseudorandom systems</td></tr>
   </table>
+  </div>
   <p>The practical rule of thumb: if predicting the "random" output would give someone a meaningful advantage or represent a security concern, standard pseudorandom generation is the wrong tool, and a cryptographically secure random number generator (a specifically designed PRNG variant resistant to this kind of prediction) or true hardware-based randomness is required instead. For virtually everything else — picking a random name from a list, rolling virtual dice, shuffling a playlist, generating a sample dataset — standard pseudorandom generation is entirely appropriate and indistinguishable from true randomness for any practical purpose.</p>
 
