   <p>"Interpolation" is the technical term for how software fills in pixel values that don't directly correspond to an original pixel — which happens constantly during resizing, since a new target size almost never lines up perfectly with the original pixel grid. Different interpolation methods (nearest-neighbor, bilinear, bicubic, and more sophisticated methods like Lanczos) trade off speed against visual smoothness, with more sophisticated methods generally producing softer, more natural-looking results at a small computational cost. Browsers handle this automatically as part of the Canvas API's image drawing behavior, using a reasonably high-quality method by default — you don't need to choose an interpolation algorithm yourself, but understanding that this estimation is happening explains why heavily enlarged images consistently look less sharp than their smaller or natively-large counterparts, regardless of which specific tool or software performed the resize.</p>
 
