   <h2>Why Phone Photos Turn Sideways</h2>
   <p>This is one of the most common photo frustrations, and it's rarely a real problem with the photo itself. Phone cameras don't actually rotate the stored pixel grid when you tilt the phone to take a photo in portrait or landscape — instead, they record an orientation flag in the image's EXIF metadata, and well-behaved software reads that flag to display the photo correctly regardless of how the raw pixels are stored. Some upload systems, older software, and specific platforms don't read this metadata correctly, displaying the photo in its raw stored orientation instead — which is why the exact same file can look correctly oriented in one app and sideways in another. Rotating the image directly, rather than relying on metadata, permanently fixes this regardless of which software eventually displays it, since the fix lives in the actual pixel arrangement rather than a flag some piece of software might ignore.</p>
 
   <h2>Rotate vs. Flip: Two Different Operations</h2>
-  <table class="bp-table">
