     <tr><td>Flip Horizontal</td><td>Mirrors the image left-to-right, like a mirror reflection</td><td>Correcting a "selfie mirror" effect, or reversing an image's direction</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Flip Vertical</td><td>Mirrors the image top-to-bottom</td><td>Less common, but useful for certain design or diagram needs</td></tr>
   </table>
+  </div>
   <p>A frequent point of confusion: rotating 180 degrees and flipping both horizontally and vertically produce the same visual result for a symmetric composition, but they're computed differently and can behave differently on asymmetric images with text or directional elements — if text needs to stay readable, rotation preserves its orientation correctly while a flip would mirror it backward. Understanding this distinction up front avoids the trial-and-error of applying the wrong operation and having to undo it.</p>
 
