   <p>Sideways or misrotated pages are disproportionately a scanned-document problem rather than a native-digital-document problem, and the reason comes down to how scanning physically works. A digital PDF exported directly from a word processor or design tool is created with the correct orientation baked in from the start — there's no physical paper involved, so there's no opportunity for an orientation mismatch. Scanning, by contrast, involves physically placing paper onto a scanner bed or feeding it through a scanner, and the resulting digital orientation depends entirely on how that physical paper happened to be positioned at the moment of capture. Multi-page documents scanned in batches, especially double-sided ones requiring the stack to be flipped partway through, introduce multiple opportunities for a page's physical orientation not to match the rest of the document, compounding across every page in the batch.</p>
 
