   <p>These are genuinely different operations, and it's worth being clear about which one you actually want before reversing anything. Character reversal treats the entire input as one continuous sequence and flips it end to end, producing text that reads backward at the letter level — the classic "mirror text" effect. Word-order reversal instead keeps every individual word completely intact and unreversed, but reorders the sequence of words themselves, which produces a very different, often still partially readable result compared to full character reversal, since each individual word remains recognizable even though the overall sentence structure is scrambled.</p>
 
