   <h2 id="why-differ">Why Word Counts Differ Between Tools</h2>
   <p>This is genuinely one of the more common points of confusion for anyone who has ever compared results across two different word counting tools on the exact same text. The core reason is that "word" doesn't have one single, universally agreed-upon technical definition — different tools make different, reasonable choices about edge cases, and those choices compound across a full document into a noticeably different total. Hyphenated compounds ("well-known") might be counted as one word by one tool and two by another. Numbers, standalone punctuation marks, and certain special characters get handled inconsistently across different counting implementations. Even something as simple as multiple consecutive spaces between words can occasionally trip up a naive counting algorithm if it's not specifically designed to collapse them correctly before counting. None of these tools are "wrong" exactly — they're each applying a defensible, internally consistent definition of what counts as a word, they just don't all apply the same one, which is exactly why the discrepancy shows up more the longer and more varied the text is, since more edge cases naturally appear across a longer document than a short, simple sentence.</p>
 
   <h2 id="char-count">Character Count: With Spaces vs. Without</h2>
-  <table class="bp-table">
+  <div class="table-wrap">
+    <table class="table">
     <tr><th>Measurement</th><th>What's Included</th><th>Common Use</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Characters with spaces</td><td>Every character including all whitespace</td><td>Platforms and forms that count total input length literally</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Characters without spaces</td><td>Only letters, numbers, and punctuation</td><td>Contexts focused specifically on actual content length</td></tr>
   </table>
+  </div>
   <p>This distinction matters most for anything with a strict character limit, since the two counts can differ by a meaningful margin for longer text — a paragraph with a hundred words easily contains close to a hundred spaces between them, meaning the "with spaces" count can run roughly 15-20% higher than the "without spaces" count for typical English prose. Always check which specific count a platform's stated limit refers to before assuming your text fits, since a piece of writing that fits comfortably under a without-spaces limit might exceed a with-spaces limit of the same numeric value.</p>
 
