   <p>Many, though not all, regions of the world observe daylight saving time, shifting their clocks forward in spring and back in autumn, typically to make better use of daylight hours during warmer months. The genuine complication for cross-timezone scheduling is that different regions change their clocks on different dates, and some regions don't observe daylight saving at all, meaning the time difference between two specific locations isn't fixed year-round — it can shift by an hour at certain points in the calendar depending on which regions are involved and their respective daylight saving schedules. A meeting scheduled for "9 AM your time, which is 7 PM mine" during one part of the year can require a genuinely different local time on one side once one region's daylight saving shift takes effect while the other region's doesn't move at the same time, which is exactly the kind of quiet scheduling trap that catches people off guard for recurring international meetings spanning a daylight saving transition.</p>
 
   <h2 id="scheduling">Scheduling Across Timezones Correctly</h2>
