     <tr><td>Using calendar software with timezone-aware scheduling</td><td>Best — automatically adjusts for daylight saving changes on both ends</td></tr>
   </table>
+  </div>
   <p>For a one-time event, checking a live world clock at the moment of scheduling is generally sufficient and reliable. For anything recurring — a standing weekly meeting spanning multiple timezones — relying on calendar software that stores the event with proper timezone awareness (rather than a fixed, hardcoded time difference) is considerably more reliable, since the software automatically recalculates the correct local time for each participant as daylight saving transitions occur on either end, rather than requiring anyone to manually remember and adjust for the shift. This distinction matters most for teams and relationships spanning regions with mismatched daylight saving calendars, since a meeting time that felt correct when first scheduled can silently drift by an hour for one participant the moment their region's clocks change while the other region's don't move on the same date.</p>
 
