   <p>In metric units, BMI is calculated as weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared (kg/m²). In imperial units, the formula is adjusted with a conversion factor: 703 × weight in pounds, divided by height in inches squared. Both formulas produce the same BMI value for the same person — they're just different ways of expressing the same underlying weight-to-height-squared ratio.</p>
 
   <h3>Understanding BMI Categories</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Below 18.5 — Underweight:</strong> may indicate insufficient nutrition or an underlying health condition.</li>
     <li><strong>18.5–24.9 — Normal weight:</strong> generally associated with the lowest health risk for most adults.</li>
     <li><strong>25–29.9 — Overweight:</strong> associated with increased risk of certain health conditions.</li>
@@ -180,7 +195,7 @@
   <p>BMI was originally developed in the 1830s as the "Quetelet Index" and later adopted by the World Health Organization as a population-level screening tool because it only needs two easily measured inputs — height and weight — and correlates reasonably well with body fat percentage across large groups of people. Its simplicity is exactly why it's used in clinics, insurance assessments, and public health surveys worldwide, even though it has known limitations for individuals.</p>
 
   <h3>BMI for Different Life Stages</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Adults (20+):</strong> the standard 18.5–24.9 "normal" range used by this calculator applies to adult men and women equally; it does not adjust for sex.</li>
     <li><strong>Older adults:</strong> some health guidelines suggest a slightly higher BMI may be acceptable for people over 65, since a bit of extra reserve can be protective during illness.</li>
     <li><strong>Children and teens:</strong> BMI for growing children is interpreted using age- and sex-specific percentile charts rather than the fixed adult categories shown here, so this calculator is intended for adult use.</li>
@@ -193,7 +208,7 @@
   <p>If you lift weights regularly, track your BMI trend over time alongside a body fat percentage measurement rather than relying on BMI alone. A rising BMI from added muscle is a very different outcome than a rising BMI from added fat, and the two can look identical on this calculator. Many trainers use BMI only as a quick baseline check at intake, then switch to body fat percentage or progress photos for ongoing tracking.</p>
 
   <h3>Common Mistakes When Checking BMI</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Entering height in the wrong unit:</strong> switching unit systems without updating both height and weight fields gives a meaningless result — always double check both fields after toggling units.</li>
     <li><strong>Treating BMI as a diagnosis:</strong> a single BMI reading is a screening number, not a medical diagnosis; unusual results are worth discussing with a healthcare provider rather than acting on alone.</li>
     <li><strong>Ignoring trend over time:</strong> a single BMI snapshot is less useful than tracking it every few months alongside other health markers.</li>
