   <h2>Currency Converter With Custom Exchange Rate</h2>
   <p>This currency converter lets you convert between any two currencies using an exchange rate you provide, rather than relying on a live data feed from a third-party financial API. This design makes the tool fast, reliable, and completely private — there's no dependency on an external service that could be rate-limited, go down, or require an API key.</p>
 
@@ -147,7 +162,7 @@
   <p>Live currency APIs are useful, but they come with trade-offs: they require constant internet access to a third-party service, are subject to rate limits or downtime, and often require an API key that isn't practical to expose in a purely client-side tool. By instead letting you enter the current exchange rate directly — which you can look up from your bank, a financial news site, or any currency data provider you trust — this tool stays fast, works reliably, and gives you full transparency over exactly which rate is being used in the calculation, rather than a rate that updates silently behind the scenes.</p>
 
   <h3>How to Use This Converter</h3>
-  <ol class="howto-list">
+  <ol>
     <li>Look up the current exchange rate between your two currencies from a reliable source (your bank, XE, Google Finance, or similar).</li>
     <li>Enter the currency codes (like USD, EUR, INR, GBP) for reference.</li>
     <li>Enter the exchange rate as "how many units of the target currency equal 1 unit of the source currency."</li>
@@ -167,7 +182,7 @@
   <p>The exchange rate shown by your bank, a card network, a money-changer, and a financial site like XE or Google Finance are rarely identical. Banks and card issuers typically add a margin on top of the interbank "mid-market" rate, which is the raw rate financial data sites usually display. Because this tool lets you type in whichever rate you're actually working with, you get an accurate result for your specific situation instead of a generic mid-market figure that may not match what you'll actually pay or receive.</p>
 
   <h3>Common Mistakes When Converting Currency Manually</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Using the inverse rate by accident</strong> — entering the INR-to-USD rate when you meant USD-to-INR produces a wildly wrong result; the Swap button exists specifically to avoid this.</li>
     <li><strong>Mixing up decimal points</strong> — some currency pairs involve rates like 0.012 or 83.45, and a misplaced decimal can throw off a calculation by orders of magnitude.</li>
     <li><strong>Forgetting the rate changes daily</strong> — exchange rates fluctuate constantly, so a rate looked up yesterday may no longer be accurate for today's conversion.</li>
@@ -186,34 +201,31 @@
   <p>Freelancers and small businesses billing in a foreign currency often need to show a client both the foreign-currency amount and its local-currency equivalent at the time of invoicing. Entering the day's rate here gives a quick, verifiable reference figure to include alongside an invoice, without needing accounting software or a spreadsheet formula just for one conversion.</p>
