   <h2>Free Coin Flip and Dice Roller</h2>
   <p>This tool provides both a simple coin flip (heads or tails) and a multi-dice roller (1-6 standard six-sided dice), each with a running history log so you can track previous results. Useful for games, decision-making, tabletop gaming, or settling friendly disputes.</p>
 
   <h3>Common Uses</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Making quick decisions</strong> — using a coin flip to choose between two options.</li>
     <li><strong>Tabletop and board games</strong> — rolling dice for games that need randomization without physical dice.</li>
     <li><strong>Teaching probability</strong> — demonstrating randomness and probability concepts with visible results and history.</li>
@@ -128,7 +151,7 @@
   <p>A running history log serves a few practical purposes beyond simple novelty. For games that require tracking cumulative results — like keeping score across several rounds, or verifying no one's claiming a different outcome than what actually happened — having a visible, timestamped-in-order log settles any dispute instantly. It's also useful for anyone demonstrating basic probability concepts, since watching the actual distribution of heads/tails or dice totals accumulate over many trials is a more convincing illustration of statistical concepts like the law of large numbers than any single flip or roll could be on its own.</p>
 
   <h3>Common Situations Where This Tool Helps</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Settling a quick decision</strong> between two options when neither side has a strong preference.</li>
     <li><strong>Board games and tabletop RPGs</strong> that call for six-sided dice, without needing to dig out physical dice.</li>
     <li><strong>Teaching basic probability</strong> in a classroom setting, using visible results and a history log as a live demonstration.</li>
@@ -146,27 +169,40 @@
   <p>A coin flip is the natural choice whenever a decision genuinely has just two outcomes — yes or no, option A or option B, who goes first. A dice roll fits better when there are more than two possibilities, or when you specifically need a numeric outcome rather than a binary one — choosing among six options, generating a random score, or simulating a specific game mechanic that calls for a six-sided die. Reaching for the right tool for the actual decision at hand, rather than defaulting to whichever is more familiar, keeps the randomization matched to how many genuinely different outcomes the situation calls for.</p>
 
