     <h2>What Is a Token in AI Language Models?</h2>
     <p>A token is the basic unit of text that a language model actually reads and generates. Models don't process raw characters or whole words one at a time — they work with tokens produced by a tokenizer, which breaks text into a sequence of sub-word pieces drawn from a fixed vocabulary learned during training. A common English word like "the" or "is" is often a single token, while a longer or less frequent word, a made-up word, a number, or a piece of code might be split into two, three, or more tokens. Every request you send to an AI model and every response it generates is measured and billed in tokens, not in words or characters, which is why understanding roughly how many tokens your text contains matters before you send it anywhere.</p>
 
@@ -252,7 +219,7 @@
     <p>Token-based pricing means the cost of a request scales directly with how many tokens it contains. Rather than hardcoding specific vendor prices into this tool — which would quickly become outdated as providers adjust their pricing — the cost estimator lets you type in whatever rate you currently know, per 1,000 or per 1,000,000 tokens, and instantly multiplies it by the estimated token count of your pasted text. This keeps the calculator useful regardless of which provider or model you're planning to use.</p>
 
