     <h2>What Is Image Metadata?</h2>
     <p>Most photos taken on a phone or digital camera carry more information than just the pixels you see. This extra information is called metadata, and the most common format for storing it in photos is EXIF (Exchangeable Image File Format). EXIF data sits in a dedicated segment inside the JPEG file, separate from the actual image content, and can include the camera make and model, the date and time the photo was taken, camera settings, the orientation of the shot, and in many cases, the precise GPS coordinates of where the photo was captured.</p>
     <p>This information is added automatically by most phones and cameras, usually without the person taking the photo noticing. It's useful for photo organization apps that sort pictures by location or date, but it also means a photo file can carry details you may not intend to share when you post or send it.</p>
 
     <h2>Why Remove EXIF Data?</h2>
     <p>There are several practical, everyday reasons people choose to strip metadata from a photo before sharing it:</p>
-    <ul class="feature-list">
+    <ul>
       <li><strong>Location privacy:</strong> a photo with GPS coordinates reveals exactly where it was taken, which may be a home address, workplace, or other location you'd rather not attach to a public post.</li>
       <li><strong>Device information:</strong> camera make and model data can reveal what equipment or phone you use, which is sometimes irrelevant to share alongside the image itself.</li>
       <li><strong>Timestamps:</strong> the exact date and time a photo was taken is sometimes more detail than needed, particularly for images being archived, submitted, or published later.</li>
@@ -229,7 +204,7 @@
 
