     <h2>What Is JSON?</h2>
     <p>JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight, text-based data format used almost everywhere in modern software: API responses, configuration files, application settings, data exports, and the output of countless tools and scripts. It represents data as nested objects (key-value pairs in curly braces) and arrays (ordered lists in square brackets), with values that are strings, numbers, booleans, null, or further nested objects and arrays. Its simplicity is exactly why it's so widely used — and also why a single missing comma or stray quote can make an entire file unreadable to a parser.</p>
 
     <h2>Why JSON Becomes Invalid</h2>
     <p>JSON has strict, unforgiving syntax rules. Unlike some configuration formats that tolerate minor inconsistencies, a JSON parser will reject the entire document over a single misplaced character. The most common causes of invalid JSON are:</p>
