     <h2>What Is Markdown?</h2>
     <p>Markdown is a lightweight plain-text formatting syntax created to let people write formatted documents using nothing but a plain text editor. Instead of clicking toolbar buttons to make text bold or insert a heading, you type a small set of symbols — a <code>#</code> before a line for a heading, asterisks around a word for emphasis, a dash for a list item — and a Markdown processor turns that plain text into properly structured HTML. Because it reads almost like plain prose even before conversion, Markdown has become the default writing format for README files on code hosting platforms, technical documentation, developer blogs, static site generators, note-taking apps, and chat platforms that support rich text.</p>
 
@@ -270,7 +215,7 @@
     <p>The converter runs a hand-written parser entirely in your browser, in two stages. The first stage scans the Markdown line by line to identify block-level structures — headings, fenced code blocks, blockquotes, horizontal rules, list blocks, table blocks, and ordinary paragraphs. The second stage processes the inline text inside each non-code block — applying bold, italic, inline code, and link or image formatting — while special characters inside code blocks and inline code spans are escaped so that angle brackets and ampersands in your sample code show up as visible text rather than being interpreted as HTML tags. Everything happens synchronously with no network request, so the live preview updates as soon as you click convert, and your content never leaves the page.</p>
 
