   <h2>About GST in India And Why Use Our Tool</h2>
   <p>Goods and Services Tax (GST) is an indirect tax system levied on the supply of goods and services in India. It was implemented on July 1, 2017, to replace multiple central and state taxes with a more unified taxation structure.</p>
   <p>GST in India generally follows four major slabs: 5%, 12%, 18%, and 28%. Essential goods may fall under lower or zero tax slabs, while common goods and services usually come under 12% or 18%, and luxury or sin goods may attract 28% GST.</p>
@@ -323,7 +255,7 @@
   <p>Under India's GST structure, a sale within the same state splits the tax equally between the central government (CGST) and the state government (SGST) — so an 18% rate becomes 9% + 9%. A sale across state lines instead charges IGST at the full rate, collected by the central government and apportioned to the destination state. This calculator shows both splits at once in the breakdown table so you can read off whichever applies to your transaction without re-running the calculation.</p>
 
   <h3>Who Uses a GST Calculator</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Small business owners and shopkeepers</strong> who need to quote GST-inclusive retail prices or verify supplier invoices.</li>
     <li><strong>Freelancers and consultants</strong> invoicing clients who need to add 18% GST on professional services.</li>
     <li><strong>Accountants and bookkeepers</strong> cross-checking CGST/SGST/IGST splits on a large batch of invoices.</li>
@@ -340,62 +272,61 @@
   <h3>Runs Entirely in Your Browser</h3>
   <p>All calculations happen client-side using JavaScript the moment you click Calculate — no amount, rate, or result is sent to any server. This makes it safe to use for quick checks on real invoice figures, client billing amounts, or business pricing without that data leaving your device.</p></section>
 
