+<section class="page-section prose">
   <h2>About the JSON Formatter Tool</h2>
   <p>The Logrith JSON Formatter beautifies, validates, minifies, and sorts JSON data instantly in your browser. Paste in compact or malformed JSON and get back properly indented, readable output — or catch a syntax error with the exact character position where parsing failed. This is one of the most frequently needed tools for anyone working with APIs, configuration files, or structured data.</p>
 
   <p>JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is the most widely used format for exchanging structured data between systems, especially in web APIs. In its raw form, especially when returned by an API without formatting, JSON often arrives as one long unbroken line of text — technically valid but nearly impossible to read or debug by eye. This tool adds proper indentation and line breaks, turning that dense block into a clearly structured, nested view that's easy to scan and understand.</p>
 
   <h3>Core Functions This Tool Provides</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Beautify (format).</strong> Adds indentation and line breaks to compact JSON, making nested objects and arrays easy to read and navigate visually.</li>
     <li><strong>Minify.</strong> Removes all unnecessary whitespace, producing the smallest possible valid JSON string — useful for reducing payload size before sending data over a network.</li>
     <li><strong>Validate.</strong> Checks whether the JSON is syntactically correct, and if not, reports exactly where and why parsing failed.</li>
@@ -172,12 +189,24 @@
   <p>While formatted JSON is better for human reading, minified JSON has its own purpose: reducing file size. When JSON is being sent over a network — as an API request or response, or embedded in a web page — every unnecessary whitespace character adds to the payload size and, at scale, to bandwidth and loading time. Minifying strips all of that extra whitespace while keeping the data itself completely unchanged, producing a smaller file that parses identically to the formatted version. Development and debugging typically call for formatted JSON; production and network transfer typically call for minified JSON.</p>
 
   <h3>Step-by-Step: How to Format JSON</h3>
