   <p>JWTs gained widespread adoption largely because they're self-contained and stateless — a server can verify a token's validity using just the signature and the secret key, without needing to look up a session record in a database for every single request. This makes JWTs particularly well suited to distributed systems and microservice architectures, where multiple independent services need to verify a user's identity without all querying a shared central session store. The tradeoff is that a JWT's claims, once issued, can't easily be revoked before expiration without additional infrastructure, which is a consideration worth understanding alongside the convenience this tool helps you inspect and debug.</p>
