   <p>One of the cleverer design decisions behind Morse code is that it isn't arbitrary — the most frequently used letters in English were deliberately assigned the shortest sequences. "E," the most common letter in English, is a single dot. "T," the next most common, is a single dash. Less frequent letters like "Q," "X," and "Z" get longer, more complex sequences. This frequency-based design meant that, on average, a message would take less time to transmit by hand than if letters were assigned sequences randomly, which mattered enormously back when every signal had to be tapped out manually by a trained operator.</p>
 
   <h3>Where Morse Code Is Still Used</h3>
