   <p>Binary is the native language of computer hardware, but raw binary numbers get unwieldy very quickly — a single byte (8 bits) written in binary is 8 characters long, but the same value in hexadecimal is only 2 characters. This is exactly why hex and octal exist as "human-friendly" shorthand for binary: because their bases are powers of 2 (16 = 2⁴, 8 = 2³), converting between them and binary is mechanically simple — you can convert hex to binary or binary to hex digit-by-digit without doing full decimal arithmetic, which isn't true for decimal.</p>
 
