@@ -130,19 +158,20 @@
   <p>Beyond preventing deliberate tampering, writing a number in words also protects against simple transcription or printing errors. A faint or smudged digit on a printed check could be misread as a different number, but the same ambiguity rarely occurs with a fully spelled-out word, since a misread letter is far less likely to transform one number word into a different, equally valid one. This is part of why formal legal and financial documents often include both the numeric and the written form side by side — if the two ever disagree, the written-word form is typically treated as authoritative specifically because it's harder to alter or misread, giving both parties a reliable way to catch and resolve a discrepancy before the document is finalized.</p>
