   <p>A common instinct is to pick obscure or unusual words to feel more secure, but this misunderstands where a passphrase's strength actually comes from. Security comes from the total search space an attacker would need to try — which depends on how many words are randomly selected and how large the word list is, not on whether any individual word feels exotic. Using unusual words instead of common ones doesn't meaningfully increase security if drawn from the same size word list; what matters is adding another random word to the phrase, since each additional word multiplies the total combinations by the full size of the word list.</p>
 
