   <h2>About the Password Strength Checker</h2>
   <p>The Logrith Password Strength Checker analyzes a password's length, character variety, and common patterns to estimate how resistant it would be to guessing and brute-force attacks — entirely within your browser, with nothing ever transmitted anywhere. It also estimates realistic crack times under different attack scenarios, giving a concrete sense of how long a given password would actually hold up.</p>
 
   <p>Password strength isn't just about following a vague rule like "use a mix of letters and numbers." The actual security of a password comes down to how many possible combinations an attacker would need to try before guessing it correctly, and that number is shaped by both length and character variety — with length mattering considerably more than most people realize.</p>
 
   <h3>What This Tool Measures</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Length.</strong> The single biggest factor in password strength — each additional character multiplies the total possible combinations.</li>
     <li><strong>Character variety.</strong> Whether the password uses uppercase letters, lowercase letters, digits, and special symbols, each of which expands the pool of possible characters at each position.</li>
     <li><strong>Entropy.</strong> A measurement, in bits, of how unpredictable the password is overall, calculated from its length and character set size.</li>
@@ -144,50 +160,59 @@
   <p>This tool estimates how long different types of attacks would realistically take to guess your password. An "online attack" scenario assumes an attacker is guessing through a live login form, which is typically rate-limited to around 1,000 attempts per second by the target service's security measures. An "offline fast hash" scenario assumes an attacker has somehow obtained a database of hashed passwords and is cracking them offline using powerful GPU hardware, capable of roughly 10 billion guesses per second for weaker hash algorithms like unsalted MD5. The difference between these two numbers is enormous, which is exactly why a password that feels "secure enough" against a login form could still be dangerously weak if the underlying password database were ever breached.</p>
 
   <h3>Practical Tips for Stronger Passwords</h3>
