     <li><strong>Merging scanned pages</strong> that were captured as separate files into one continuous document.</li>
@@ -160,11 +179,12 @@
   <p>It's worth pausing to consider whether merging is actually the right move for a given situation. Some contexts specifically call for a single file — an online application form that only accepts one upload, a printing job that needs to run as one continuous job, or an email where attaching one file is cleaner than several. Other contexts are better served keeping files separate, such as when each document needs to be referenced, edited, or shared independently later. Merging is a one-way convenience: once combined, you'd need to split the file again to separate the original documents, so it's generally worth keeping untouched copies of your original files before merging, just in case you need them individually again later.</p>
