   <h2>How PDF to JPG Conversion Works</h2>
   <p>This tool uses PDF.js, the same open-source rendering engine that powers Firefox's built-in PDF viewer, to draw each page of your PDF onto an HTML5 canvas at a resolution you choose. Once a page is rendered onto the canvas, the browser's native image encoding converts that canvas into a JPG or PNG file, ready to download. Because rendering happens through the same engine used to display PDFs in a browser, text, vector graphics, and embedded images all come through with high fidelity — nothing is approximated or simplified.</p>
 
@@ -147,7 +178,7 @@
   <p>The two output formats serve different purposes. JPG uses lossy compression, which produces smaller files and works well for pages containing photographs or continuous-tone images, where minor compression artifacts are rarely noticeable. PNG uses lossless compression and supports transparency, which matters for PDF pages containing sharp text, line art, logos, or diagrams where compression artifacts around hard edges would be more visible — and essential if the original content relies on a transparent background. As a rule of thumb, scanned photo-heavy pages usually look fine as JPG, while text-heavy or graphic-heavy pages benefit from PNG's lossless precision.</p>
 
   <h3>Common Situations Where This Conversion Is Useful</h3>
