     <li><strong>\d</strong> matches any digit (0-9), while <strong>\D</strong> matches any non-digit character.</li>
     <li><strong>\w</strong> matches any word character (letters, digits, underscore), while <strong>\W</strong> matches anything that isn't.</li>
     <li><strong>\s</strong> matches whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines), while <strong>\S</strong> matches non-whitespace.</li>
@@ -161,7 +174,7 @@
   <p>Parentheses in a regex pattern create a capture group, which extracts a specific portion of each match rather than just confirming that a match occurred. For example, the pattern (\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2}) matching a date like "2024-03-15" would capture "2024" as Group 1, "03" as Group 2, and "15" as Group 3 — letting you pull out the year, month, and day separately rather than just the full matched string. This tool displays every capture group for every match, making it easy to confirm your groups are extracting exactly the parts of the text you intend to use.</p>
 
   <h3>Common Use Cases for Regex</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Form validation</strong> — checking that an email address, phone number, or postal code matches an expected format before accepting it.</li>
     <li><strong>Data extraction</strong> — pulling specific pieces of information, like dates or prices, out of larger blocks of unstructured text.</li>
     <li><strong>Find and replace</strong> — searching for a pattern across a codebase or document and replacing every occurrence with a different format.</li>
