   <p>When a regex doesn't match what you expect, work through it step by step rather than guessing at the whole pattern at once. Start by simplifying the pattern down to its smallest matching piece and confirm that works, then add complexity back one piece at a time, checking after each addition. Pay close attention to characters that have special meaning in regex — a period (.) matches any character unless escaped as \., and parentheses, brackets, and braces all carry structural meaning rather than being literal characters. Forgetting the global flag is one of the most common reasons a pattern only seems to match once when multiple matches were expected, so if you're only seeing one result where you expected several, that's usually the first thing to check.</p>
