   <p>The key difference is what the interest is calculated on. Simple interest is always calculated on the original principal only, so the interest earned each year is identical. Compound interest, by contrast, is calculated on the principal plus all previously accumulated interest, so the amount of interest earned grows each period. Over short time periods or small amounts, the difference between the two is minor, but over longer periods, compound interest produces significantly more growth (or, for a loan, significantly more cost).</p>
 
