   <h2>About the Text Diff Tool</h2>
   <p>The Logrith Text Diff tool compares two blocks of text and highlights exactly what changed between them — additions in green, removals in red — so you can spot differences instantly instead of reading through both versions line by line looking for discrepancies. It supports comparison at the line, word, or character level, and works entirely in your browser.</p>
 
   <p>Comparing two versions of text comes up constantly: checking what changed between two drafts of a document, reviewing edits a collaborator made, comparing two versions of code before merging, or verifying that a copied block of text matches the original exactly. Doing this manually by reading both versions side by side is slow and error-prone, especially with longer text, which is exactly the problem this tool solves.</p>
 
   <h3>Three Levels of Comparison</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Line by Line.</strong> Compares entire lines at once, showing which lines were added, removed, or changed. This is the most useful mode for comparing code, configuration files, or any text organized into discrete lines.</li>
     <li><strong>Word by Word.</strong> Breaks each line down into individual words and highlights exactly which words changed within a line, useful for catching small edits in prose that a line-level diff would mark as an entire line changing.</li>
     <li><strong>Character by Character.</strong> The most granular mode, highlighting individual characters that differ — useful for catching typos, punctuation changes, or very small edits that word-level comparison might group together.</li>
@@ -156,7 +169,7 @@
   <p>This tool uses a longest common subsequence (LCS) algorithm, a well-established approach to text comparison that also powers version control tools like Git's diff feature. Rather than naively comparing text position by position — which breaks down the moment anything is inserted or deleted partway through, shifting everything after it out of alignment — LCS finds the longest sequence of matching content shared between the two texts, then treats everything outside that shared sequence as either an addition or a removal. This is why inserting a single new line in the middle of a document doesn't cause the diff tool to mark every subsequent line as changed; it correctly identifies that only the new line is different and everything else still matches.</p>
 
   <h3>Common Use Cases for Text Diffing</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Reviewing document edits.</strong> Quickly see what a collaborator or editor changed in a shared document without manually comparing two long files.</li>
     <li><strong>Comparing code snippets.</strong> Check differences between two versions of a function or configuration file, especially useful when working outside a version control system.</li>
     <li><strong>Verifying copied text.</strong> Confirm that a block of text was copied exactly, with no accidental changes, truncation, or formatting differences introduced.</li>
@@ -168,50 +181,59 @@
   <p>The case-sensitive toggle controls whether "Hello" and "hello" are treated as identical or different during comparison. For most natural-language prose comparison, keeping case sensitivity off can reduce noise from inconsequential capitalization differences. For code comparison, however, case sensitivity usually should stay on, since many programming languages treat identifiers, variable names, and keywords as case-sensitive — a difference between myVariable and myvariable could represent an actual bug, not just a stylistic variation.</p>
 
