   <h2>About the URL Encoder/Decoder Tool</h2>
   <p>The Logrith URL Encoder converts text into a URL-safe format using percent-encoding, and decodes it back into readable text. This is essential whenever special characters, spaces, or non-ASCII text need to be safely included in a URL, query string, or API request without breaking the structure of the URL itself.</p>
 
@@ -127,7 +154,7 @@
   <p>JavaScript provides two related but different encoding functions, and picking the right one matters. encodeURI is designed for encoding a complete URL — it leaves structural characters like /, ?, &amp;, and # untouched, since those characters have special meaning in the overall URL structure and shouldn't be encoded if you're encoding an entire address. encodeURIComponent, by contrast, is designed for encoding a single component or value — like one query parameter's value — and it does encode those structural characters, since within a single value, a literal & or ? should be treated as data rather than as part of the URL's structure. As a general rule, use encodeURIComponent when encoding a value that will be placed inside a query string, and reserve encodeURI for encoding an entire URL that already has its structure in place.</p>
 
   <h3>Common Situations That Require URL Encoding</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Passing user input as a query parameter.</strong> If a search term contains spaces or special characters, it needs to be encoded before being appended to a URL as a parameter value.</li>
     <li><strong>Building API request URLs.</strong> Many APIs require specific values, like API keys or search terms, to be properly encoded within the request URL.</li>
     <li><strong>Creating redirect URLs.</strong> A URL used as a redirect target, especially one that itself contains a query string, often needs to be encoded to avoid the outer URL misinterpreting the nested one.</li>
@@ -138,12 +165,24 @@
