   <h3>Privacy</h3>
   <p>All encoding and decoding happens locally in your browser using JavaScript's built-in functions — nothing you enter is sent to a server or stored. This makes the tool safe to use even with sensitive query values, API parameters, or tokens you don't want passing through a third-party service.</p>
@@ -152,39 +191,36 @@
   <p>One issue that trips up developers regularly is double encoding — accidentally encoding a string that's already been encoded once, which turns the percent signs from the first encoding pass into their own encoded form (%25) and produces a broken, unreadable result when decoded incorrectly or passed to a system expecting single encoding. This typically happens when a value gets encoded both on the client side and again on the server side, or when a URL is constructed by concatenating an already-encoded parameter into a string that then gets encoded again as a whole. If a decoded URL still contains %25 sequences or other garbled percent signs, double encoding is usually the cause, and the fix is to decode it twice, or better, to trace back through the code and remove the redundant encoding step.</p>
