   <h2>About the UUID Generator Tool</h2>
   <p>The Logrith UUID Generator creates random, version 4 UUIDs (Universally Unique Identifiers) instantly in your browser — one at a time or in bulk batches of up to 100 at once. UUIDs are widely used in software development as unique identifiers for database records, API resources, session tokens, and distributed systems, and this tool covers generation, formatting, and validation from a single page.</p>
 
@@ -122,7 +152,7 @@
   <p>There are several UUID versions defined by the standard, each generated using a different method. Version 1 UUIDs incorporate the generating computer's MAC address and a timestamp, which means they can theoretically be traced back to the machine that created them. Version 4, which this tool generates, is built entirely from random or pseudo-random numbers instead, with no embedded timestamp or hardware identifier. This makes version 4 UUIDs the most common choice for general-purpose unique identifiers today, since they reveal nothing about when or where they were generated and don't require any special input data beyond a good source of randomness.</p>
 
   <h3>Why UUIDs Are Used Instead of Sequential IDs</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Unpredictability.</strong> Sequential IDs like 1, 2, 3 let anyone guess neighboring record IDs just by incrementing a number in a URL. UUIDs can't be guessed this way.</li>
     <li><strong>No coordination needed.</strong> Multiple servers, databases, or services can generate IDs independently without checking with each other to avoid collisions, which matters a lot in distributed systems.</li>
     <li><strong>Safe to expose publicly.</strong> Because UUIDs don't reveal how many records exist or what order they were created in, they're safer to use in public-facing URLs and APIs than sequential database keys.</li>
@@ -130,7 +160,7 @@
   </ul>
 
   <h3>Common Uses for UUIDs in Development</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Database primary keys</strong> — especially in distributed databases or systems with multiple write nodes.</li>
     <li><strong>API resource identifiers</strong> — giving each resource (user, order, document) a unique, non-guessable ID in REST or GraphQL APIs.</li>
     <li><strong>Session and authentication tokens</strong> — generating unique session identifiers that are hard to predict or brute-force.</li>
@@ -151,38 +181,35 @@
   <p>When designing a new database table or API, picking between a UUID and a simple auto-incrementing integer ID is a common early decision. Auto-increment IDs are smaller, slightly faster to index in some databases, and easy to read at a glance. UUIDs trade some of that simplicity for unpredictability and the ability to generate IDs across multiple servers without coordination. A common middle-ground approach used by many modern systems is to keep an internal auto-increment ID for database performance while exposing a UUID externally in URLs and API responses, getting the benefits of both. Which approach fits best depends on the scale and structure of the system being built, but for anything distributed, public-facing, or merge-sensitive, UUIDs are usually the safer default.</p>
 </section>
 
