+<section class="page-section prose">
   <h2>Convert Between YAML and JSON</h2>
   <p>This tool converts YAML documents to JSON, or JSON to YAML, handling nested objects and arrays. It's useful for developers working with configuration files, Kubernetes manifests, CI/CD pipelines, and APIs that use either format.</p>
 
@@ -132,7 +144,7 @@ address:
   <p>Both YAML and JSON represent the same underlying data structures — objects (key-value mappings) and arrays (ordered lists) — but with different syntax. JSON uses curly braces, square brackets, and quoted keys, making it precise and easy for programs to parse. YAML uses indentation and minimal punctuation instead, which many developers find easier to read and write by hand, especially for configuration files, at the cost of being more sensitive to exact indentation.</p>
 
   <h3>Where YAML Is Commonly Used</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Configuration files</strong> — many modern tools (Docker Compose, GitHub Actions, Ansible) use YAML for configuration.</li>
     <li><strong>Kubernetes manifests</strong> — Kubernetes resource definitions are written almost exclusively in YAML.</li>
     <li><strong>CI/CD pipelines</strong> — build and deployment pipeline definitions are frequently written in YAML.</li>
@@ -149,7 +161,7 @@ address:
   <p>This converter reads YAML line by line, using each line's indentation level to figure out where it sits in the overall structure. When indentation increases, the parser treats that as a nested object or list; when it decreases, it closes out the current nesting level and returns to a shallower one. List items are recognized by a leading hyphen, and each key-value pair separated by a colon is interpreted either as a scalar value or the start of a new nested block depending on whether anything follows the colon on the same line.</p>
 
   <h3>Which Value Types Are Recognized</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li><strong>Booleans</strong> — the literal words <code>true</code> and <code>false</code> convert to JSON booleans.</li>
     <li><strong>Null values</strong> — <code>null</code>, <code>~</code>, or an empty value convert to JSON <code>null</code>.</li>
     <li><strong>Numbers</strong> — unquoted numeric values convert to JSON numbers rather than strings.</li>
@@ -167,14 +179,14 @@ address:
   <p>This converter supports the YAML features most configuration files actually use: nested mappings, lists, and the standard scalar types. It does not support YAML anchors and aliases (<code>&amp;</code>/<code>*</code> references for reusing blocks), multi-document files separated by <code>---</code>, or flow-style inline collections like <code>{key: value}</code> written on a single line. If your YAML file relies on these advanced features, a dedicated YAML library will handle them more reliably.</p>
 
   <h3>Common Mistakes</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li>Mixing tabs and spaces for indentation — YAML (and this parser) relies on consistent spacing, and a stray tab can shift an entire block to the wrong nesting level.</li>
     <li>Forgetting a trailing comma check in JSON mode — valid JSON never has a trailing comma after the last item in an object or array.</li>
     <li>Assuming quoted numbers stay as numbers — a YAML value like <code>"30"</code> in quotes is intentionally kept as a string, not converted to the number 30.</li>
   </ul>
 
   <h3>Frequently Searched Terms</h3>
-  <ul class="feature-list">
+  <ul>
     <li>yaml to json online converter</li>
     <li>convert kubernetes yaml to json</li>
     <li>json to yaml formatter</li>
@@ -189,34 +201,31 @@ address:
   <p>A common pattern among developers is pasting a YAML config that's behaving unexpectedly into this tool, converting it to JSON, and visually comparing the nesting against what was intended. Because JSON's braces make every level of nesting explicit, it's often faster to spot a misplaced key or an array that accidentally became an object than it is by staring at raw YAML indentation.</p>
 </section>
 
