+<section class="page-section prose">
+<h2>What these guides cover</h2>
+<p>Logrith's AI-assisted tools, the Resume Analyzer and the Website Speed Analyzer, produce scores and suggestions, and a score is only useful if you know what sits behind it. Each guide here explains what the tool actually inspects, which signals it weights most, and which of its suggestions are worth acting on first.</p>
+<h2>How to read an automated score</h2>
+<p>An automated score is a summary, not a verdict. A resume score reflects structure, keyword coverage and clarity; it cannot judge whether your experience fits a particular role. A speed score reflects measurable load behaviour on one test run; it can swing between runs because networks and servers vary. Treat the number as a starting point, fix the highest-impact items first, then test again.</p>
+<h2>Common mistakes</h2>
+<ul>
+<li>Chasing a perfect score instead of fixing the two or three issues that matter most.</li>
+<li>Running a single test and treating the result as fixed. Run it several times and compare the trend.</li>
+<li>Pasting sensitive documents into tools without checking where processing happens. Each guide states how the tool handles your data.</li>
+</ul>
+<h2>Where to go next</h2>
+<p>If you are comparing options, start with the guide for the tool you plan to use, then browse the <a href="/categories/ai-tools">full AI tools category</a>. New guides are added as new tools are released and existing ones are updated when a tool's behaviour changes.</p>
+</section>
+
 </main>
 
